Thanks Rames — this review drove three follow-up commits on this PR. All four observations are addressed:

**3A — SW vs GPU H.265 split documented.** Added an explicit "Limitations" entry in [`docs/guides/hdr.mdx`](https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes/blob/vance/chunk-encoder-hdr/docs/guides/hdr.mdx) (commit `86830a9b`):

> **GPU H.265 emits color tags but no static mastering metadata** — `useGpu: true` with HDR (nvenc, videotoolbox, qsv, vaapi) tags the stream with BT.2020 + the correct transfer (smpte2084 / arib-std-b67) but does **not** embed `master-display` or `max-cll` SEI. ffmpeg does not let those flags pass through hardware encoders. The output is suitable for previews and authoring but not for HDR10-aware delivery (Apple TV, YouTube, Netflix). For spec-compliant HDR10 production output, leave `useGpu: false` so the SW `libx265` path embeds the mastering metadata.

So a downstream caller wiring `useGpu: true` + `hdr: { transfer: "pq" }` and expecting full HDR10 will now hit this in the docs before they hit it in production.

**3B — mixed-transfer comment landed at the call site.** Added in `videoFrameExtractor.ts` (commit `86830a9b`):

\`\`\`ts
// dominantTransfer is "majority wins" — if a composition mixes PQ and HLG
// sources (rare but legal), the minority transfer's videos get converted
// with the wrong curve. We treat this as caller-error: a single composition
// should not mix PQ and HLG sources, the orchestrator picks one transfer
// for the whole render, and any source not on that curve is normalized to
// it. If you need both transfers, render two separate compositions.
\`\`\`

I went with the comment-and-document path rather than hard validation because the orchestrator already picks one transfer for the whole render (so legitimate callers can't trip this), but the comment now exists for any future caller bypassing the orchestrator.

**3C — type safety.** You're right that the `if (config.hdr)` sites all branch the same way for `undefined` vs `false` (both falsy), so behavior is unchanged. Followed that thread one more layer: ended up landing on `hdr: undefined` rather than `hdr: false` (commit `baa30d18`) because the engine field is actually typed `{ transfer: HdrTransfer } | undefined`, not `| false` — the PR description mis-stated the target shape. `undefined` is the sentinel callers already use throughout the codebase for SDR. Tests still green.

**libx264 + HDR landmine — closed.** Took the warn-and-strip path in `chunkEncoder.ts` rather than throwing, since strip+warn keeps existing call sites compiling and the SDR/BT.709 output is at least internally consistent (commit `86830a9b`):

\`\`\`ts
// libx264 cannot encode HDR. If a caller passes hdr with codec=h264 we'd
// produce a "half-HDR" file (BT.2020 container tags but a BT.709 VUI block
// inside the bitstream) which confuses HDR-aware players. Strip hdr and
// log a warning so the caller picks h265 (the SDR-tagged output is honest).
if (options.hdr && codec === "h264") {
  console.warn(
    "[chunkEncoder] HDR is not supported with codec=h264 (libx264 has no HDR support). " +
      "Stripping HDR metadata and tagging output as SDR/BT.709. Use codec=h265 for HDR output.",
  );
  options = { ...options, hdr: undefined };
}
\`\`\`

Half-HDR artifact is impossible from this code path now — the worst case is an SDR/BT.709 file with a logged warning. Test matrix in `chunkEncoder.test.ts` updated to cover the rejection branch (the previous "h264 + HDR tags BT.709" assertion is now "h264 + HDR strips HDR and tags BT.709 with a warning").

## Review feedback follow-up

@jrusso1020's followup is addressed: the orchestrator now enforces mutual exclusivity between `--crf` and `--video-bitrate` for programmatic callers that bypass the CLI guard.

`packages/producer/src/services/renderOrchestrator.ts` (≈line 1512):

```ts
// CLI overrides (--crf, --video-bitrate) flow through job.config and must
// win over the preset-derived defaults. The CLI enforces mutual exclusivity
// upstream, but we still resolve them defensively. Without this, the flags
// are silently ignored at the encoder spawn sites below — see PR #268 which
// dropped the prior baseEncoderOpts wiring.
//
// Programmatic callers can construct RenderConfig directly and bypass the
// CLI's mutual-exclusivity guard. If both are set we honor crf (matches the
// CLI semantics where --crf is the explicit override) and warn loudly so
// the caller doesn't get a quietly-different bitrate than they passed in.
if (job.config.crf != null && job.config.videoBitrate) {
  log.warn(
    `[Render] Both crf=${job.config.crf} and videoBitrate=${job.config.videoBitrate} were set. ` +
      `These are mutually exclusive; honoring crf and ignoring videoBitrate. ` +
      `Set only one to silence this warning.`,
  );
}
const effectiveQuality = job.config.crf ?? preset.quality;
const effectiveBitrate = job.config.crf != null ? undefined : job.config.videoBitrate;
```

JSDoc on the `RenderConfig` interface was also updated to document the mutual exclusivity (`/** Mutually exclusive with `videoBitrate`. */` and `/** Mutually exclusive with `crf`. */`) so the contract is visible to programmatic callers via type hints.

