# Render quantizes all GSAP/DOM motion to a hardcoded 30fps grid, so `--fps 60` produces 30fps motion in a 60fps container

## Summary

When rendering with `--fps 60`, the output `.mp4` is a correct 60fps CFR file, but **all GSAP/DOM-driven motion only updates 30 times per second** — every other frame is a duplicate. WebGL shader transitions are unaffected and play at a true 60fps, which makes the regression very visible: shader moments are buttery, every plain scene stutters.

The cause is that the runtime re-quantizes every render seek to a **hardcoded `canonicalFps = 30`**, regardless of the requested render fps. There is no attribute, CLI flag, or env var that changes it.

This does not show up in the Studio preview, because preview plays in real time off `requestAnimationFrame` and never goes through the render-seek path.

## Environment

- Repo: `heygen-com/hyperframes` @ commit `0c1e236d`
- `@hyperframes/core` `0.7.10` (also reproduces with the CLI's bundled `0.7.6` runtime)
- CLI: `npx hyperframes render . --fps 60 --quality high`
- macOS (Chrome/ANGLE-Metal) — but the cause is platform-independent (pure seek math), so Docker/Linux are affected identically.

## Reproduction

Any composition whose motion is a GSAP timeline registered on `window.__timelines`. A linear tween makes it unambiguous:

```html
<div data-hf-id="m" id="main" data-composition-id="main" data-width="1080" data-height="1920" data-start="0" data-duration="4">
  <div data-hf-id="b" id="box" style="position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:200px;height:200px;background:#fff"></div>
</div>
<script>
  var tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
  // Linear vertical drift: at a true 60fps every frame must differ.
  tl.to("#box", { y: 1700, duration: 4, ease: "none" }, 0);
  window.__timelines = window.__timelines || {};
  window.__timelines["main"] = tl;
</script>
```

```bash
npx hyperframes render . -o out.mp4 --fps 60
```

### Expected

Every one of the 240 frames is a distinct position (≈0.83px/frame). Motion matches the Studio preview.

### Actual

The box advances only every **other** frame: frames (0,1) identical, (2,3) identical, … → 120 distinct positions in a 240-frame file = 30fps motion.

## Evidence / how to verify

Per-frame luma delta on a continuously-moving section exposes the cadence (mpdecimate/framehash are unreliable here — a static grain overlay and font anti-aliasing make duplicate frames hash-distinct):

```bash
ffmpeg -i out.mp4 -vf "select='between(n,195,240)',signalstats,metadata=print:key=lavfi.signalstats.YDIF" \
  -an -f null - 2>&1 | grep -oE 'YDIF=[0-9.]+'
```

- **GSAP scene:** `0.63, 0.001, 0.90, 0.002, 1.04, 0.004, 1.08, 0.001, …` — alternating move / ~0 → **2:1 frame doubling (30fps)**.
- **Shader transition** (same file): `5.30, 4.54, 5.39, 4.28, 4.94, …` — every frame moves → **true 60fps**.

The `~0.00x` values are not exact zeros; they are the grain overlay's sub-pixel dither on an otherwise-frozen frame, which is why naive duplicate-frame detectors miss it.

## Root cause

The render-seek path floors the requested time onto the `canonicalFps` grid, and `canonicalFps` is a hardcoded `30` with no setter anywhere in production code.

1. The producer hands the page the correct, already-fps-quantized frame time and calls `window.__hf.seek(t)`:
   - `packages/producer/src/services/fileServer.ts:534-540`
     ```js
     hf.seek = function(t) {
       p.renderSeek(t);                                  // ← seeks GSAP timeline (re-quantized below)
       var nextTimeMs = (Math.max(0, Number(t) || 0)) * 1000;
       if (window.__HF_VIRTUAL_TIME__ && typeof window.__HF_VIRTUAL_TIME__.seekToTime === "function") {
         window.__HF_VIRTUAL_TIME__.seekToTime(nextTimeMs); // ← exact time → shader/rAF clock (60fps OK)
       }
       seekSameOriginChildFrames(window, nextTimeMs);
     };
     ```

2. `renderSeek` re-quantizes that exact time to `canonicalFps`:
   - `packages/core/src/runtime/player.ts:209-227` (`renderSeek`, reads `deps.getCanonicalFps()` at `:211`, quantizes at `:224`)
   - `packages/core/src/runtime/player.ts:78-87` (`seekTimelineDeterministically` → `quantizeTimeToFrame(timeSeconds, canonicalFps)` at `:83`)

3. The quantizer floors onto the grid:
   - `packages/core/src/inline-scripts/parityContract.ts:35`
     ```ts
     export function quantizeTimeToFrame(timeSeconds: number, fps: number): number {
       const safeFps = Number.isFinite(fps) && fps > 0 ? fps : 30;
       const safeTime = Number.isFinite(timeSeconds) && timeSeconds > 0 ? timeSeconds : 0;
       const frameIndex = Math.floor(safeTime * safeFps + 1e-9);
       return frameIndex / safeFps;
     }
     ```
   For output frame `k` at time `k/60`: `quantizeTimeToFrame(k/60, 30) = floor(k/2)/30` → frames `(0,1) → 0`, `(2,3) → 1/30`, … i.e. exact 2:1 duplication.

4. `canonicalFps` is **only ever `30`**:
   - `packages/core/src/runtime/state.ts:101` — `canonicalFps: 30` (the only assignment in production code; the sole non-test write to `state.canonicalFps` is in `state.test.ts`)
   - `packages/core/src/runtime/init.ts:1923` — `getCanonicalFps: () => state.canonicalFps`
   - `init.ts` parses `data-width/height/duration/start/end` but **no fps attribute**; nothing wires the render `--fps` into `state.canonicalFps`.

### Why the shader path is exempt
`hf.seek` advances `__HF_VIRTUAL_TIME__` to the **exact** `t` (`fileServer.ts:537-538`); shaders/`requestAnimationFrame` sample that clock and so move at the true render fps. Only the GSAP master timeline goes through the 30fps-quantized `renderSeek`.

### Dead config (worth removing or wiring up)
The producer injects `window.__HF_EXPORT_RENDER_SEEK_CONFIG` (`mode`, `step: 1/120`, `offsetFraction: 0.5`) at `packages/producer/src/services/fileServer.ts:297-326`, but **nothing consumes it** — the only other reference is a string entry in `packages/core/src/compiler/htmlDocument.ts:16`. It looks like the scaffolding for sub-frame render seeking was started but never connected, which is likely the intended home for the fix.

## Impact

- Every render above 30fps silently delivers 30fps motion for non-shader content. The container fps, frame count, and file size all look correct, so it is easy to miss.
- Determinism itself is fine (the quantization is stable) — the grid is just pinned to the wrong value.

## Workaround (composition-side, no runtime changes)

For anyone blocked today: after the original `hf.seek(t)` runs, re-seek the GSAP master to the exact `t`. This keeps shader/media/deterministic-adapter sync intact and restores true 60fps DOM motion. (No-op in the Studio preview.)

```js
(function enableExact60fpsRenderSeek() {
  function wrapSeek(fn) {
    if (typeof fn !== "function" || fn.__hfExact60) return fn;
    var wrapped = function (t) {
      var r = fn.apply(this, arguments);
      var master = window.__timelines && window.__timelines["main"];
      if (master) { try { master.pause(); master.time(Math.max(0, Number(t) || 0), false); } catch (e) {} }
      return r;
    };
    wrapped.__hfExact60 = true;
    return wrapped;
  }
  function trapHf(obj) {
    if (!obj || obj.__hfExact60Trapped) return;
    obj.__hfExact60Trapped = true;
    var stored = wrapSeek(obj.seek);
    Object.defineProperty(obj, "seek", {
      configurable: true, enumerable: true,
      get: function () { return stored; },
      set: function (fn) { stored = wrapSeek(fn); },
    });
  }
  var held = window.__hf;
  if (held) trapHf(held);
  try {
    Object.defineProperty(window, "__hf", {
      configurable: true, enumerable: true,
      get: function () { return held; },
      set: function (v) { held = v; trapHf(v); },
    });
  } catch (e) {}
})();
```

## Suggested fix direction

Drive `canonicalFps` from the actual render fps (the producer already knows it — e.g. `quantizeTimeToFrame(checkpointSec, fps)` at `packages/producer/src/parity-harness.ts:239`) instead of pinning it to `30`, so the render-seek grid matches the output grid. Quantization (determinism) is preserved; only the grid resolution changes.

@Breno-Oliveira10 — shipped! A browser ESM build of the validator just landed in **v0.7.17** as the `@hyperframes/lint/browser` entry (#1773).

It runs the rule engine **fully client-side** — no Node.js, no `fs`, no server round-trip:

```ts
import { lintHyperframeHtml, shouldBlockRender } from "@hyperframes/lint/browser";

const result = await lintHyperframeHtml(htmlString, { filePath: "index.html" });
// result.ok, result.findings — validate before any preview/network call
```

A few details:

- **Node-free by construction.** The browser entry exposes `lintHyperframeHtml`, `lintMediaUrls`, and `shouldBlockRender` — everything that operates on an HTML string. It's built with a `platform: "browser"` pass, so the build *fails* if any `node:` builtin sneaks in; a full esbuild bundle (deps inlined) comes out to ~558 KB with **zero node builtins**.
- **`lintProject` stays Node-only.** The directory-walking project linter still lives on the main `@hyperframes/lint` entry (it needs the filesystem) — only the HTML-string path is exposed to the browser.
- **Leaner install too.** `@hyperframes/lint` now depends only on `@hyperframes/parsers` (we dropped `@hyperframes/core`), so it no longer transitively pulls `studio-server`/`hono` or other unrelated packages — just the validator.

That should close the authoring-side of the loop you described for browser-only contexts. The browser-side *render* piece (#1661) is still its own track. Thanks for the detailed write-up — it made the scope easy to land cleanly.

