```

So a clip carrying `style="background-image: url(../assets/bg.png)"`, or a `<style>` block with a `url()`, loses the rebase on paste exactly the same way an `<img src>` does. A fix that rebases only `PATH_ATTRS` would close one of three doors.

**And the rebase runs in both directions, so `../` isn't the whole case.** With the `assetExists` probe supplied (the preview supplies it; `rewriteSubCompPaths.ts:79-85`):

```ts
if (!needsRewrite(relativePath)) {
  if (!assetExists) return relativePath;
  ...
  const sibling = resolve("/", join(compDir, filePart)).slice(1);
  return assetExists(sibling) ? sibling + suffix : relativePath;
}
```

A plain sibling ref — `<link href="_shared.css">` next to `design/styleframes/frame-01.html` — is rebased *up* to `design/styleframes/_shared.css` in the preview. The saved file keeps `_shared.css`, so pasting into `index.html` writes `_shared.css` and 404s at the root. No `../` involved. The docblock at `:37-50` calls this out as the styleframe-thumbnail bug it was added to fix, so it's a live layout, not a hypothetical.

If you take the `rewriteAssetPath` approach, it needs the same `assetExists` probe the preview uses or it can't tell case (1) from case (2) — and the probe is filesystem-backed, which the browser doesn't have. That may be the argument for Rames's second option (take path-bearing attributes from the live element), since the live DOM already has every one of these three surfaces resolved correctly by a process that *could* see the disk.

