Thanks. Both are fixed at `c2d80766`. An independent review of the first fix found two more cases of the same kind, and the fix now goes to their shared root.

1. **An older save's recovery overwrote a newer gesture.** The root was ordering. An attribute save read the file before it joined that file's write queue. So an older save with a slow read could land after a newer one, and a recovery read could settle between a newer save's start and its write. `persistElementAttribute` now joins the queue first, then reads, patches and writes inside it. Saves land in the order they start, and a failed save's recovery read waits behind any save already started. On top of that ordering, each lane remembers its newest save. A save records its value in the lane store only if no newer save has started. It moves the preview only while it is the newest save and no drag is live. That rule also covers its own optimistic patch and failure unwind, which used to write the preview around the check. A drag refused while a save is pending puts its before-value back, and the pending save's result then lands on screen. On success, the store records the value that was saved, not whatever the DOM held. The lane rule is `createLiveLanes` in `timelineEditingHelpers.ts`, and the clip and group saves both use it.

   New tests in `useAutomationLanes.save.test.tsx`, each asserting that file, preview and store agree once everything settles:
   - your A/B/C case;
   - an older save with a slow read, then a newer save that saves, fails or is refused by recording, on both the clip and group lanes;
   - an older failed save whose read-back fails while a newer save lands;
   - a newer live drag keeps its preview while an older save's recovery lands, and a newer release keeps its preview while an older save fails under it;
   - a drag refused between an older failure and a pending save, on both lanes, with the pending save either landing or failing.

   All six slow-read cases fail on `25d49f34`, the head before the queue change. I also removed each guard from a copy of the code: the start-order queueing, the newest-save check, and each half of the check on preview moves. Each removal fails at least one test.
2. **Pastes on two clips could mark the wrong paste.** A paste now marks its span only if three things still hold when its save resolves: it is the latest paste, its clip is still selected, and the selection is unchanged. Tests cover pastes on two clips resolving in order and in reverse (the latest paste is marked either way) and a paste whose clip was left before it saved.

The typecheck passes, and so do 4721 tests across the player, hooks, contexts, editor components and utils.

One related case stays out of this PR because it was already there before it: cancelling an edge-stretch mid-drag only re-previews the lane and leaves its drag marker behind. A follow-up PR routes the lane's cancel through the same revert.

